---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
const steps = [
  ['You type a domain', 'Your browser needs an IP address, so it asks the operating system\u2019s stub resolver.'],
  ['Local cache check', 'The OS checks its own cache. A recent answer is returned instantly, no network trip.'],
  ['Recursive resolver', 'Your configured resolver (e.g. 1.1.1.1 or your ISP\u2019s) receives the question and checks its cache next.'],
  ['Root servers', 'On a miss, the resolver asks a root server: \u201cwho handles .com?\u201d It gets back the .com TLD servers.'],
  ['TLD servers', 'The resolver asks the .com servers: \u201cwho is authoritative for example.com?\u201d It gets back your nameservers.'],
  ['Authoritative answer', 'Your DNS host\u2019s server returns the actual record, the A record\u2019s IP, with a TTL.'],
  ['Cached and returned', 'The resolver caches the answer for the TTL and hands the IP to your browser, which connects.'],
];

---
<BaseLayout title="How DNS Works: The Step-by-Step Resolution Walkthrough" description="How DNS resolution actually works: caches, recursive resolvers, root and TLD servers, authoritative answers, and TTLs, the complete walkthrough."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"], ["How DNS Works"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">How DNS Works</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">How DNS Works</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      Typing a URL and getting a page feels instant. Behind it, a question travels through up to four
      layers of the internet and comes back, usually in under 50 ms. Here is the whole journey.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-two-kinds-of-dns-servers">The two kinds of DNS servers</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ttl-why-answers-stick-around">TTL: why answers stick around</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-can-break-at-each-step">What can break at each step</a></li>
        <li><a href="#keep-going">Keep going</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <Diagram name="resolution" caption="The DNS resolution path: your question travels through resolver, root/TLD, and authoritative servers, with caching at every hop" />

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-3">
      {steps.map(([title, desc], i) => (
        <div class="flex gap-4 rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-lg bg-gradient-to-br from-accent to-violet font-display text-sm font-bold text-canvas">{i + 1}</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-ink">{title}</p>
            <p class="mt-0.5 text-sm text-ink-dim">{desc}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="rich-text mt-12">
      <h2 id="the-two-kinds-of-dns-servers">The two kinds of DNS servers</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Recursive resolvers</strong> do the asking, they walk the tree on your behalf and cache
        answers. <strong>Authoritative servers</strong> do the answering, they hold the actual records
        for specific zones. A resolver might serve millions of users; an authoritative server serves
        the domains it hosts. Our <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a> works by
        asking several independent resolvers the same question and comparing their cached answers.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ttl-why-answers-stick-around">TTL: why answers stick around</h2>
      <p>
        Every record carries a <strong>time-to-live</strong>, the number of seconds a resolver may
        keep serving the cached answer. A 3600-second TTL means a change can take up to an hour to
        appear anywhere; a 300-second TTL means minutes. This caching is why the system scales, and
        also why <a href="/dns-propagation/">propagation</a> isn't instant.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-can-break-at-each-step">What can break at each step</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cache stale</strong> → old IP served; fix: wait out the TTL or <a href="/flush-dns-cache/">flush your local cache</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Delegation broken</strong> → registry NS records don't match your zone; check with a record lookup on the NS type.</li>
        <li><strong>Record missing</strong> → NXDOMAIN; the name doesn't exist in the authoritative zone.</li>
        <li><strong>Resolver failure</strong> → SERVFAIL; try another resolver in our <a href="/dns-speed-test/">speed test</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="keep-going">Keep going</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/what-is-dns/">What is DNS?</a>, the foundations</li>
        <li><a href="/dns-records/">DNS record types</a>, the full reference</li>
        <li><a href="/dns-record-lookup/">Try a live lookup</a></li>
      </ul>

    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
